**Hypotheses

**Programme Understanding & Conversion**

* We believe that replacing the limited poster-only experience with a dedicated digital platform will help users better understand what Movement to Work is and what it offers.
* We believe that clearly communicating the two-week programme, key benefits and expected outcomes within the hero section will increase the likelihood that users continue exploring the platform.
* We believe that prominent calls to action such as "Explore the Programme" and "Speak to Your Work Coach" will make the next step clearer and increase expressions of interest in Movement to Work.
* We believe that communicating the programme benefits before asking users to take action will make users more confident about joining.

**Programme Timeline**

* We believe that displaying the two-week programme as a visual timeline will reduce uncertainty about what participants will do and make the programme easier to understand.
* We believe that connecting each programme activity with the skill or benefit gained will help users recognise the value of completing the programme.

**Interactive Questionnaires**

* We believe that interactive questionnaires will create a more engaging learning experience than presenting employability information entirely through static content.
* We believe that questionnaires focused on skills, employability and career interests will encourage users to actively think about their own circumstances while learning.
* We believe that clear progress indicators and short questionnaire stages will improve completion compared with long, unstructured questionnaires.

**Transferable Skills**

* We believe that showing transferable skills using relatable examples will help users identify skills they did not previously recognise.
* We believe that explaining how transferable skills relate to real employment situations will improve users' confidence when discussing their abilities with employers.

**CV Support**

* We believe that providing clear CV creation and improvement support will help users feel more prepared to apply for employment opportunities.
* We believe that showing users how to tailor CV content towards specific roles will make the support more useful than generic CV advice.

**Content & Navigation**

* We believe that a focused navigation structure and short content sections will reduce information overload compared with competitor websites containing large amounts of competing content.
* We believe that providing a clear next action throughout the experience will reduce dead ends and help users progress through the platform.

**Mobile & Accessibility**

* We believe that designing mobile-first will make the programme more accessible to users who do not regularly have access to a desktop computer.
* We believe that plain English, clear typography and accessible interaction patterns will make employment support easier to understand for users with varying levels of digital confidence.

**Opportunities & Next Steps**

* We believe that presenting future opportunities will help users see a clearer connection between completing Movement to Work and progressing into employment.
* We believe that clearly explaining what happens after programme completion will increase users' confidence that Movement to Work provides a meaningful pathway rather than an isolated two-week experience.

**Overall Hypothesis**

* We believe that creating a focused, accessible and interactive Movement to Work platform will improve understanding of the programme, increase engagement with employability content and encourage more eligible users to progress towards joining Movement to Work compared with relying primarily on the existing promotional poster.
